ong for the default adopter. | unsound | - |\n| 6 | correctness | tools/unattended/check-unattended.sh:3770 | medium | medium | confirmed | Introduced by this diff. pass_commit now attributes a commit by its trailer alone (lib:736), but check 23's ambiguity test still reads `%s` (check-unattended.sh:3770-3778). Take a commit with `Pass: TOOL-x-1` whose subject names a same-group sibling TOOL-x-2. It is selected as x-1's pass commit, reported as 'names two passes' through a non-failing printf, and `continue`d before ds_pend, so it is never graded or counted. At the base, the same commit would have been x-2's commit and graded. --check-commit grades the staged paths against x-1's declaration at commit time, which mitigates the case unless the commit used --no-verify. The finder's case 2, the multi-unit trailer, is weak: --check-commit requires every staged path to be covered by EACH named unit's declaration, so a commit that passed it cannot red the close. Case 1 alone is the confirmed defect. | sound | - |\n| 7 | correctness | tools/unattended/unattended.sh:9742 | medium | medium | confirmed | verb_check_commit is new in this diff and lists staged paths with `git diff --cached --name-only`. Reproduced with git 2.54: after `git mv out/f.txt in/f.txt` it prints only in/f.txt, while `git diff-index --cached --name-only HEAD` and `--no-renames` print both paths. Check 23 grades the pass commit with `diff-tree -r --name-only` (check-unattended.sh:3797), which has no rename detection, so the deleted source lands in dsout at the close. The commit-time step therefore passes a move out of an undeclared path that the close will count. The effect is contained because check 23 still catches it, but by then it can no longer be repaired, which is what this verb exists to prevent. | sound | - |\n| 8 | correctness | tools/unattended/unattended.sh:9720 | low | low | confirmed | Reproduced: for a message with `pass: TOOL-x-1`, `%(trailers:key=Pass,valueonly)` returns TOOL-x-1, because