'which has no open dispatched pass in $slug', has no arm: neither test file at e252baf contains that text. The --check-commit arms cover only the subset refusal, the no-trailer subject refusal, 'Pass: none' and the unbound branch. The refusal fires inside 'for u in $trl' before any staged path is read. A regression there would go unnoticed, but the effect is limited to a misleading hint or a missed refusal on a narrow path. | sound | - |\n| 19 | verification | tools/unattended/lib-unattended.sh:534 | medium | - | refuted | No defect is shown, only a test that does not exist. At e252baf the awk read of MAP_ROOT in resolve_generated_indexes (lib-unattended.sh ~534) handles the conf's documented restricted grammar correctly: plain KEY=VALUE, a quoted value with its quotes stripped, CR stripped, and comment lines not matched. That grammar bans inline comments and `export`, and map_lib.load_conf agrees with the awk on every form the grammar allows. The only divergence is an empty `MAP_ROOT=`, which is pathological: map_lib reads it as the repo root and the awk falls back to memory/map. The finding shows no input where the parse returns a wrong root on a reachable path. An untested but correct reader, graded medium, is refuted. | sound | - |\n| 20 | intent | tools/govkit/govkit.py:1993 | medium | medium | confirmed | At e252baf, govkit.py check 6c collects tokens with re.findall(r'\\{([a-z_]+)\\}'), so `{MAP_ROOT}`, `{map-root}` and `{root2}` are never collected and selfcheck passes the row. The shell reader skips any path that still holds a brace, and only prints a stderr line when it does. Spec 4 S5 requires 6c to refuse any token other than the three. The H-side selftest has only the missing-generator arm (aWP-4 AC5). The uppercase-token arm (`[aWP r1 M9]`) exists only in later commits, which confirms the gap at this SHA. The effect is contained: it needs a descriptor author to mis-spell a token, and the result is a skipped row and false check-23 counts, not data loss. | sound | - |\n| 21 | intent | tools/unattended/unattended.sh:9720 | medium | medium | confirmed | verb_check_commit (unattended.sh ~9720) reads `git interpret-trailers --parse \\| sed -n 's/^Pass: *//p'`, and that match is case-sensitive. I checked that interpret-trailers --parse keeps a `pass:` key in lowercase, while `%(trailers:key=...)` matches keys case-insensitively. So log_attribution_tokens, which check 23 and pass_commit read, attributes `pass: TOOL-x-1` to the unit, but --check-commit sees no trailer. When the subject does not name the unit, it returns 0 unchecked. Separately, the `*\" none \"*) return 0` case lets a message carrying both `Pass: none` and `Pass: TOOL-x-1` through. Check 23 reads that same message as PASSTRAILER none TOOL-x-1 and attributes it to TOOL-x-1. The two readers of one trailer disagree, and the repairable catch is lost. Check 23 still grades the commit at the close, so the effect is contained, and the path needs a non-canonical or mixed trailer. | sound | - |\n| 22 | intent | tools/unattended/check-unattended.sh:3886 | low | - | refuted | The per-run line is printed. It goes to the REPORT channel unless a SOLO write occurred, which is what the gate's output contract requires. The header contract says `Exit 0 + no output = clean`. Exception TWO admits the graded-and-overlapped count only together with the `check 23 SOLO` line, as a verdict-changing finding. A run whose passes were all solo and that wrote nothing outside its declarations has nothing the check could have counted or forgiven, so it is genuinely clean, not falsely covered. The S3 count stays visible under GOV_UNATTENDED_REPORT=1. This is a deliberate channel choice with no wrong result, and the finding is graded low. The proposed fix is unsound: printing the count on the default channel for every run without overlap would add output to every clean bar, which breaks the header's no-output-equals-clean contract. | unsound | - |\n| 23 | intent | .githooks/commit-msg:44 | low | low | confirmed | At e252baf, .githooks/commit-msg loops over the two kit locations and runs --check-commit only when unattended.sh and .unattended.conf both exist. When neither candidate matches, the loop ends with no message. Spec 3 S4 and section 5 say an absent kit is an announced skip, and the same hook announces every equivalent skip in its merge-audit half. The hook is gov-own, and gov tracks both the kit and .unattended.conf, so the path is reachable only in a gov checkout missing them. Check 23 still grades the commit at the close. The defect is a contained spec deviation. | sound | - |\n| 24 | intent | tools/unattended/lib-unattended.sh:807 | low | low | confirmed | Spec 2 section 5 promises that a walk with no trailer is announced once per run as legacy, and that check 23 says which attribution each run's walks used. Nothing at e252baf emits such a line: log_attribution_tokens, pass_commit and check-unattended.sh carry no attribution-mode output. The spec's own rev-2 made attribution per COMMIT rather than per walk, which makes the section-5 lines stale prose rather than a missing safety net. The finding's mis-attribution impact is mostly covered elsewhere: --check-commit S3 refuses a subject-named pass commit that has no trailer. What remains is a record that claims behaviour the code does not have. | sound | - |\n| 25 | intent | tools/unattended/check-unattended.sh:303 | low | low | confirmed | Spec TOOL-aWindowedPass-4 section 5 (line 94) says 'check 23 prints the effective set it read, with each pair's source'. At e252bafb3, check-unattended.sh:303 assigns the resolver's output to GENERATED_INDEXES. It is then read only by check 38 (line 1004) and the gen-render skip (line 3636), and nothing emits it on stdout or through report(). resolve_generated_indexes (lib-unattended.sh:527-567) prints to stderr only when it skips a row with an unresolvable token, and never says which source a kept pair came from. The promise is unmet, but it is a readiness-checklist item rather than an acceptance criterion, and the verdicts are unaffected, so the impact is low. The fix is unsound in its first branch: tagging each pair inside the resolver's output would change the 'index:generator' pair format that build_commit (lib-unattended.sh, which splits on ':' with ${_bc_i%%:*}), scan_shared_index_overlaps (check 38) and the gen-render skip all parse. The 'drop the promise from the spec' branch is sound. | unsound | - |\n| 26 | intent | tools/unattended/check-unattended.sh:3871 | low | low | confirmed | Both halves were introduced by this diff. (1) Commit 7ab7ae535 removed the UNDECLARED_WRITE_CEILING shrink-only ratchet, which the diff deletes ('fail 23 ... shrink-only ceiling admits'). The same diff then re-adds the comment 'It is COUNTED, and the ratchet below decides the verdict' at check-unattended.sh:3871. The code that now decides is the per-run zero test from TOOL-aWindowedPass-5. (2) In lib-unattended.sh at the head, the build_commit header comment ('which commit BUILT this unit ... the generator half is never excluded...') is followed directly by the new log_attribution_tokens and GEN_REGION_AWK/check_gen_region_only comments and definitions, so build_commit's contract comment now sits above unrelated code. Both are comment-only and do not change behaviour, so the severity is low. | sound | - |"
